WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.000
The blockbuster to beat all blockbusters is
finally here.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.860
Avengers: Endgame is Marvel's love letter
to its own Cinematic Universe, tying together

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:10.820
all 21 films that came before it.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:11.880
So how did it do?

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.680
From its early stumbles to its stirring finish,
here are the five worst and five best things

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.860
about Marvel's latest genre juggernaut.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.320
Warning: Massive spoilers ahead!

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.320
Infinity War is a tough act to follow, with
all the Spider-Man-dissolving and whatnot.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.300
Half the life in the MCU was just snuffed
out!

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.340
So it's no surprise that Endgame starts on
a hopeless note.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.400
But Infinity War's infamously brutal finish,
unfortunately, makes the first 20 minutes

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.580
or so of Endgame a bit of a slog.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.310
It's a glaring weak spot, especially for a
film with such an epic runtime.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.960
There's a gap between the Hawkeye-centered
cold open and the title card, in particular,

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.340
that feels stagnant, especially considering
the thrill ride to come.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.100
Part of the reason for that is trailer fatigue;
Marvel did a great job limiting promotional

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.170
footage mainly to these early moments, but
the downside to that is that the start of

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:04.610
the film felt like watching the trailers and
TV spots all over again, without enough fresh

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.060
material mixed in to make it feel like the
film had properly started.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:13.340
There are a surprising number of moving moments
in Endgame, especially considering it's arguably

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:18.030
the biggest Hollywood blockbuster action spectacle
ever conceived, with an epic battle scene

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:22.260
centerpiece that felt like it was choreographed
by a pack of bright 11-year-olds on a sugar

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:22.760
binge.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.340
There's Happy Hogan's tear-jerking hamburger
funeral talk to Tony's daughter Morgan, for

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:32.570
example, which also works on a meta level,
since Happy actor Jon Favreau directed 2008's

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.700
Iron Man, which kickstarted the MCU.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.240
The chat also hearkens back to all the hamburger
talk in Iron Man.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:42.940
But Happy's brief tender moment is just one
tiny beat in Endgame that likely had moviegoers

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:43.960
stifling sobs.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.380
There's the "sacrifice off" for the Soul Stone
between Hawkeye and Black Widow, Tony's ultimate

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:54.920
sacrifice, Peter Parker and "Mr. Stark" reuniting,
Tony connecting with his father in 1970 — the

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.520
list goes on and on.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:01.700
While Endgame certainly feels better-paced
overall than the occasionally sluggish Infinity

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.170
War, the numbers don't lie.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:08.399
At just more than three hours long, you'll
need to prepare accordingly, going easy on

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.760
the soda and maybe even prepping your posterior
with some Doctor Strange-style yoga.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:17.410
As satisfying as it was to spend this long
with our favorite MCU heroes, some of these

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:21.280
gags could have been saved for the Blu-ray,
or just left on the cutting room floor.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:25.540
There is certainly fat to be trimmed in the
trip to New Asgard (in more ways than one!),

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:28.520
or in the uneven journey to New Jersey back
in 1970.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:33.640
The Howard and Tony scenes in this timey-wimey
jaunt were effective, but this leg of the

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.840
"Time Heist" overall was arguably the weakest.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:42.100
Balancing the many moods of the MCU in one
three hour thesis statement is no mean feat.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:46.820
But the Russo brothers managed to do just
that in Endgame, skillfully blending the cool,

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:51.600
the cosmic, the bombastic, and the slapsticky
sides of this vast and daunting universe.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.160
It's also way funnier than you'd expect, considering
the circumstances these heroes are in and

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:57.280
how high the stakes are.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.680
But there's also a healthy dose of refreshing
silliness, most of it aimed at Thor, who's

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.270
sporting a beer belly and getting compared
to The Dude in The Big Lebowski by Tony Stark.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:10.590
Nevermind the Last Action Hero-style switcheroo
that would be necessary here, since Lebowski

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.400
star Jeff Bridges also played Iron Man villain
Obadiah Stane.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:15.640
"That was you!

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.360
You were in that movie!"

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:18.720
"You were in a movie?"

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:20.740
"Yes."

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.420
It's a great gag at first to see the God of
Thunder humbled in this way, compelled to

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:30.380
shirk his highfalutin' Asgardian duties for
more mortal endeavors, even if it overstays

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.180
its welcome a bit.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:32.380
Speaking of which …

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:37.480
There are some tonally erratic moments in
Endgame, with some emotional ping-ponging

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.780
between post-apocalyptic hopelessness and
cartoonishness.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.920
In other words: It's a comic book movie!

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:47.450
But one moment that flat-out just doesn't
work is when a tipsy Thor helps his buddy

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:49.500
Korg navigate a session of Fortnite.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.040
A player named "noobmaster69" is giving Korg
trouble, you see, since … Wait — what

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:56.640
movie is this again?

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.260
Look: There's nothing wrong with Fortnite
per se or including IRL pop culture in an

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.010
MCU flick — and it'll age better than, say,
the Bill Maher cameo in Iron Man 3 — but

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:10.980
these are two great tastes that simply don't
taste great together … at least not on film.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.760
The limited time Avengers/Fortnite crossover
is a blast, but the product placement in the

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.260
film was just too much.

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:23.020
With its trippy and richly rewarding time-travel
plot, Endgame feels in many ways like a "greatest

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.840
hits" supercut of our favorite MCU moments.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.660
It's a fitting tribute, since it's the last
time we'll see this particular cast of heroes

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:31.230
together in maybe forever.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:36.800
It also feels a lot like 2012's Avengers — way
more than Infinity War did — with the Whedonesque

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.540
meta-banter about Back to the Future and the
practicalities of time travel.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.199
There's also an excellent callback to the
elevator fight in 2014’s Captain America:

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:49.610
The Winter Soldier, the welcome return of
Tilda Swinton's The Ancient One from Doctor

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:54.410
Strange, the "instant kill mode" introduced
in Homecoming finally put to good use by Spider-Man,

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:55.880
and so much more.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.740
The Fortnite gag felt the most like a weak
Saturday Night Live sketch, but the whole

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.490
"What if Thor, but fat?"

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:05.480
subplot was perhaps the most grating aspect
of Endgame.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:09.740
There were certainly moments when the silliness
served to inject some levity into the famously

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:14.270
pompous Asgardian, but didn't Ragnarok do
enough to make Thor a winning comic character?

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:18.470
Chris Hemsworth is definitely up to the task
of playing this kind of broad humor, but it

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:19.160
went too far.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.860
When Thor was having a panic attack at the
sight of his mother, for example, Hemsworth

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:27.660
was playing it for laughs, but the scene was
really pretty tragic, bordering on PTSD.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:32.100
Thor had been undermined by this point by
all the "wacky fat guy" stuff; plus his character

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:36.630
arc was counter to the development he saw
in Ragnarok, when he learned there to embrace

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:37.720
his role as a leader.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.780
But now he's heading into space and bestowing
Asgardian leadership to Valkyrie?

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:46.800
It's a wacky little twist, made more believable
and palatable by Hemsworth's winning performance,

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.820
but something just feels off about it.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:54.100
Endgame culminates with an extraordinary battle
scene featuring an absurd amount of characters

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:58.930
clashing on some totally scorched Earth, like
a group of excited kids dumped a bucket of

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:03.050
Marvel action figures in the dirt and spent
the afternoon swooshing them through the air,

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:06.530
banging them together until some kind of convoluted
resolution was reached.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:07.530
It's kind of perfect!

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:12.880
It's an incredible sequence featuring a brain-melting
amount of CGI, but that hardly matters when

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.540
you're caught up in the majesty and brutality
of it all.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:20.910
It's a whirlwind of badass character intros,
crowd-pleasing triumphs, and a meme-ready

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:24.920
scene featuring the most power women in the
MCU teaming up for the first time.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:29.580
It's almost too much to take in at once, but
nothing in the MCU so far has captured the

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:33.740
epic scope of the comics or brought to life
what it would really be like if dozens of

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:36.760
super-powered folks clashed in such a high-stakes
battle.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.360
After 11 years and 22 MCU adventures, one
of the worst things about Endgame is that

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.780
it really is living up to the promise of its
title.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:50.160
Sure, the MCU will live on via Disney+, with
several characters toplining their own shows,

00:06:50.169 --> 00:06:54.360
and the big screen MCU machine will grind
on, churning out fare that's likely going

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.200
to be excellent in its own way.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:00.600
But this special blend of characters will
probably never again assemble on the big screen.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:05.840
That said, Endgame is an excellent showcase
and recap for all that the MCU Infinity Saga

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:10.120
had to offer, skillfully blending all the
elements that made this decade-plus run so

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.900
winning into an epic that's ultimately satisfying
and hard to quibble with.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:19.400
Cap's impossibly romantic time travel adventure
to "get a life" with Peggy Carter is just

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:24.509
one of many examples in Endgame of Marvel
offering a real sense of closure in a cinematic

00:07:24.509 --> 00:07:28.500
universe that's been resistant to it from
the first post-credits scene in Iron Man.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:34.040
In the 21 MCU films that preceded it, there
was always a nagging sense of "What's next?"

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:39.420
But with Endgame, Marvel finally made a film
that truly feels like the end of great saga.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:44.080
The Russo brothers, in fact, even decided
to omit a post-credits teaser this time around,

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:49.009
a controversial move that reinforces the finality
of the film's title, signalling to fans that

00:07:49.009 --> 00:07:54.150
this isn't a $350M advertisement for what's
ahead, but a proper bookend to the film that

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:54.920
started it all.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:08:00.180
We even get a triumphant "I am Iron Man!"
from Tony Stark, right before his ultimate

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:03.600
sacrifice, echoing the end of the first MCU
adventure.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:04.660
"I am Iron Man."

